{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.35,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTN", "VOUTP"],
      "observed": "R36 is labelled as a 24k resistor in the netlist and SPICE deck, but its LCSC part (MPN FRC0805J240TSD, description '...±5% 24Ω 0805') is a 24 ohm part. Value and purchased part disagree by three orders of magnitude.",
      "expected": "A 24k bleed/sense resistor across the VOUTP/VOUTN pack output should be a 24k part. If 24Ω was really intended, the value field is wrong. Either way the BOM and schematic should agree.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\")"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R36 VOUTN VOUTP 24k"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the 24Ω part is populated, a low-value resistor sits across the battery-pack output and drains roughly 1-2 A at pack voltage, which is a serious heat and energy problem. If 24k is correct, the BOM is wrong and the wrong part may be bought.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["D30", "R60"],
      "pins": ["D30.4", "D30.2", "R60.2"],
      "nets": ["ESTOP_OUT"],
      "observed": "The second LED of the dual LED D30 has its anode (A2, pin 4) on ESTOP_OUT, and its cathode (K2, pin 2) reaches ESTOP_OUT through R60. Both ends of that LED path are on the same net, so the LED has no voltage across it and cannot light. The first LED, D30 A1/K1, is driven from the LED rail and GND as expected.",
      "expected": "A status LED should have its anode and cathode on different nets, with the cathode returned to ground through its current-limit resistor, as the K1/R59 channel is.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"A2\") (pintype \"input\")) (node (ref \"J13\") (pin \"6\") ...) (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"2\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"U36\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"Y\") (pintype \"output\"))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"128\") (name \"Net-(D30-K1)\") ... (node (ref \"R59\") (pin \"1\")) ... (net (code \"129\") (name \"Net-(D30-K2)\") ... (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"1\"))"}
      ],
      "impact": "The e-stop status LED on the ESTOP_OUT channel never lights. This removes a visual e-stop indication and may hide a fault.",
      "confidence": 0.3
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U17", "U28", "U31", "U32", "U33", "J15"],
      "pins": ["U17.2", "U17.3", "U28.6", "U28.7", "U31.13", "U31.14", "U32.1", "U32.11", "U33.9", "U33.10"],
      "nets": ["SCL6", "SDA6"],
      "observed": "The ET6226M digit/key driver U17 (a CLK/DAT serial interface, not I2C) is on the same SCL6 and SDA6 nets as the I2C devices U28, U31, U32, U33 and the J15 I2C OLED header.",
      "expected": "A CLK/DAT-protocol display driver should be on its own pins, separate from the shared I2C bus, so that bus traffic and the display protocol do not interfere.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"178\") (name \"SCL6\") ... (node (ref \"U17\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"CLK\") (pintype \"input\")) ... (node (ref \"U28\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"SCL\")) ... (node (ref \"U31\") (pin \"13\") (pinfunction \"SCL\")) ... (node (ref \"U33\") (pin \"10\") (pinfunction \"SCL\"))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"180\") (name \"SDA6\") ... (node (ref \"U17\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"DAT\") (pintype \"input\")) ..."}
      ],
      "impact": "I2C transactions can toggle the display driver's clock and data lines, and display refresh can corrupt I2C traffic. This could cause sensor or ADC read errors, or display glitches.",
      "confidence": 0.25
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist is largely consistent with a plausible CM4 carrier design. The strongest finding is R36, whose 24k value conflicts with its 24Ω part and description, followed by a dead LED path in D30 and a shared CLK/DAT and I2C bus on U17. The spec does not cover these items, so the confidence is moderate."
}

